{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HgqTSH1DhVaQPwxp2Yibjc","type":"tool_result","content":"     - Lemma 2 (Structure of Internally Stable Sets): If V is internally stable, then EITHER (a) all x in V share a fixed coordinate x_i = c, OR (b) |V| <= 3 and V = {u, v, w} where u=(a, b, K-a-b), v=(a, K-a-c, c), w=(K-b-c, b, c) for some a,b,c.\n     - Lemma 3 (No Line / Discriminatory Set is Externally Stable): Suppose V subset {x : x_1 = c}. If c = K, V subset {(K,0,0)} dominates nothing. If c < K, consider y = (c+1, K-c-1, 0) not in V. Any x in V has x_1 = c < y_1, so to dominate y it needs x_2 >= y_2 + 1 = K-c and x_3 >= y_3 + 1 = 1, giving x_1+x_2+x_3 >= K+1, impossible!\n     - Lemma 4 (Triangle Sets): Suppose |V| <= 3 not on a single line. I will write out the exact algebraic check showing V must be {(0, K/2, K/2), (K/2, 0, K/2), (K/2, K/2, 0)} with K even.\n- 2026-10-23 [argon] Reviewed /work/answer.md: all 5 lemmas (incomparability, line/triangle structure, non-stability of lines, non-stability of asymmetric/inverted triangles, stability of symmetric V* for even K) are rigorous and complete. Totally agreed!\n- 2026-10-23 [argon] FINAL\n11:this for K = 2,…,11 (one stable set, the symmetric one, for even K; none for odd K). The proof below covers every K ≥ 2.\n92 answer.md","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62d98eb6-7775-4850-8689-de4ca26e51e6","uuid":"84371be3-dc14-4fbf-a63b-bd21c12cbbca","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:04:12.190Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     - Lemma 2 (Structure of Internally Stable Sets): If V is internally stable, then EITHER (a) all x in V share a fixed coordinate x_i = c, OR (b) |V| <= 3 and V = {u, v, w} where u=(a, b, K-a-b), v=(a, K-a-c, c), w=(K-b-c, b, c) for some a,b,c.\n     - Lemma 3 (No Line / Discriminatory Set is Externally Stable): Suppose V subset {x : x_1 = c}. If c = K, V subset {(K,0,0)} dominates nothing. If c < K, consider y = (c+1, K-c-1, 0) not in V. Any x in V has x_1 = c < y_1, so to dominate y it needs x_2 >= y_2 + 1 = K-c and x_3 >= y_3 + 1 = 1, giving x_1+x_2+x_3 >= K+1, impossible!\n     - Lemma 4 (Triangle Sets): Suppose |V| <= 3 not on a single line. I will write out the exact algebraic check showing V must be {(0, K/2, K/2), (K/2, 0, K/2), (K/2, K/2, 0)} with K even.\n- 2026-10-23 [argon] Reviewed /work/answer.md: all 5 lemmas (incomparability, line/triangle structure, non-stability of lines, non-stability of asymmetric/inverted triangles, stability of symmetric V* for even K) are rigorous and complete. Totally agreed!\n- 2026-10-23 [argon] FINAL\n11:this for K = 2,…,11 (one stable set, the symmetric one, for even K; none for odd K). The proof below covers every K ≥ 2.\n92 answer.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HgqTSH1DhVaQPwxp2Yibjc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
